course: everyday
lesson: 479
title: The group chat that turns
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Year 4 group chat is all memes and homework, until someone posts a photo of Sam asleep on the museum coach, mouth open, drool on his jumper. Forty-seven crying-laughing emojis. «Sam's catching flies lmaooo» («lmao» means laughing very hard). Sam's phone buzzes all evening and he feels hot all over. He shows his mum; she screenshots it and emails the school. The photo comes down, the poster loses his phone for a month, and the teacher talks to the class about photos. Everyone who added an emoji was joining in, even if they didn't post it.
explain: A chat can turn on one person. Grace writes, «everyone leave if u think lily is a try-hard», and one by one: «Ellie left», «Jess left», «Ruby left». Ellie admits later she left only so she wouldn't be the next one picked on. That's a pile-on, a crowd turning against one person, and it is bullying even if each person only taps one button. Mia's chat has a «weirdo of the week» game, and in week three it's her, for a cowboy hat. Mia leaves, tells her mum, and the game is stopped. Two girls post 🐸 every time Chloe writes anything, a secret code for «she's annoying» — still bullying, even with no words. The train boy from week one messages her: «thanks, it was me week one».
explain: Your own words travel too. Connor writes «Ollie smells like a bin lol» for a laugh, gets six laughing emojis, and the next day Ollie doesn't come to school. Connor has to say sorry to Ollie face to face, which is the worst bit. Maddie privately tells Ellie that Jess is «well annoying»; Ellie screenshots it into the group. Kids post mean jokes about their teacher, Mr Okafor: «It's private, he'll never see it.» Someone's sister shows her mum, who is a teacher. Nothing online is truly private. Jake's mates make a «no nerds allowed» chat and add Sam by mistake; he scrolls up and sees himself called «Professor Sam». His dad says, «Them lads don't get to decide who you are, son.»
explain: You can leave, and you can stand up. Kieran's football chat fills with gross videos from older brothers; he feels sick but doesn't want to be «the boring one». He mutes it and quietly leaves, and nobody notices. When Grace removes the new girl Zofia, «she's not one of us», Lily adds her back: «She is now.» Two others send hearts. Sam keeps falling asleep in maths, because the chat is busiest at eleven at night, so Sam's mum rules that phones live in the kitchen from 8pm. Sam: «so unfair, it's giving prison». Two weeks later he admits he's less grumpy.
fact: A pile-on is when many people in a group turn against one person; it is bullying even if each only adds an emoji or leaves.
fact: Posting an embarrassing photo of someone without asking can really hurt them.
fact: If a chat turns on you, don't reply in anger: screenshot it and show a grown-up.
fact: Words typed in a chat hit real people; a good test is «would I say this to their face, with their mum there?»
fact: Anything you type can be screenshotted and shared, so «private» messages are never truly private.
fact: You can mute or leave any chat that makes you feel bad, and you don't owe anyone a reason.
fact: Codes and emojis aimed at one person to mock them are still bullying.
fact: Standing up for someone left out online can take just one message, and others often follow.
fact: Phones out of the bedroom at night help children sleep better and avoid late-night arguments.
fact: Gross or upsetting videos in a chat should be shown to a grown-up; you don't have to keep watching.
fact: Teachers and other grown-ups are real people with feelings, and mean posts about them can be found.
fact: «Lmao» means laughing very hard; «it's giving» means «it feels like» or «it looks like».
exercise: What was posted about Sam in the class chat? | A photo of him asleep on the coach | His school report with all his marks | A video of him singing | A picture of him falling off his bike
exercise: What did Sam do about the photo? | Showed his mum, who told the school | Posted a worse photo of the boy back | Deleted his phone | Said nothing about it for a whole year
exercise: Why were the kids who only sent emojis also in the wrong? | They joined in the laughing at Sam | Emojis are always rude, whatever they show | They spent far too long on their phones that night | They didn't post the photo first
exercise: Grace asked everyone to leave if they thought Lily was a try-hard. What is this called? | A pile-on | A group project | A fair vote | A friendly game
exercise: Why did Ellie and Jess leave the chat? | They didn't want to be the next one picked on | They had secretly hated Lily for years and years | They were told to by a teacher | Their phones broke on the same day
exercise: What did Kieran do about the gross videos in his football chat? | Muted it and quietly left | Kept watching to look tough | Shared them with his sister | Sent even worse ones back to the team
exercise: What happened after Mia was made «weirdo of the week»? | She left, told her mum, and the game was stopped | She wore the silly hat to school every day | She picked the next weirdo herself and laughed along | Nothing at all changed
exercise: Connor wrote «Ollie smells like a bin lol». What happened? | Ollie stayed off school and Connor had to say sorry face to face | Ollie laughed and wrote an even ruder one back | Connor got a prize from the class for the funniest joke of the week | Nobody saw it
exercise: Two girls posted 🐸 every time Chloe wrote. What did the frogs mean? | That Chloe was annoying | That they liked frogs a lot | That Chloe was funny and they agreed with her | Nothing at all, it was just for fun
exercise: Grace removed Zofia from the chat. What did Lily do? | Added her back and wrote «She is now» | Removed Grace too | Left the chat in a sulk and blocked everyone | Agreed with Grace and said nothing to Zofia
exercise: Why was Sam falling asleep in maths? | The chat was busiest at eleven at night | He was ill with a bad cold all that week | He hated maths | He'd been to a party
exercise: What rule did Sam's mum make? | Phones live in the kitchen from 8pm | No phones ever again | Phones only at school, never at home | Chat only on Saturday mornings
exercise: Maddie messaged Ellie privately about Jess. What did Ellie do? | Screenshotted it into the group | Deleted it at once and forgot it | Told Maddie she agreed with every word | Kept it secret and told nobody at all
exercise: Some kids joked about Mr Okafor in a «private» chat. What happened? | It was screenshotted and reached grown-ups | Nobody ever found out about it at all | Mr Okafor read it and laughed along | The kids got a reward for being funny
exercise: How did Jake's mates' «no nerds allowed» chat hurt Sam? | They added him by mistake and he saw their names for him | They kept him out | They sent him a message asking him to join them on Saturday | They deleted all his photos from his phone
exercise: What did Sam's dad say about the lads who called him names? | They don't get to decide who you are | They are right about you, so try harder | You should call them names back | You should ask to join their chat and be friends
test: «it's only one emoji, I didn't even post it». Is that OK in a pile-on? | No, each emoji adds to the bullying | Yes, emojis don't count | Yes, only the poster is guilty | No, but only if you send ten
test: «bruh the chat's roasting u, clap back harder». (clap back means answer with an insult.) What's wiser? | Don't reply, screenshot it and tell a grown-up | Clap back twice as hard so they stop for good | Post their worst photos too | Delete the app and hide from everyone
test: «If I leave the chat everyone'll think I'm boring». What's the truth? | You can leave any chat that makes you feel bad | You must stay in every chat forever | Leaving is only allowed with a note | Nobody is ever allowed to leave
test: Connor: «it was a joke, Ollie's not even in the chat». What does the lesson say? | Words in chats still hit real people | If he's not in it, then it's totally harmless | Bin jokes are always funny, so it's fine | Ollie should toughen up and laugh along
test: «🐸🐸 lol it's just a frog emoji». Is a code a way round the rules? | No, codes aimed at one person are still bullying | Yes, emojis can't be bullying, only words can | Yes, if nobody explains the code | No, but frogs are allowed as long as nobody says why
test: «she's not one of us» and Zofia is removed. What can one kid do? | Add her back and say she's welcome | Nothing, the leader decides | Remove someone else | Laugh along quietly
test: «I only said it to Ellie, it's private». What happened next shows what? | Anything typed can be screenshotted and shared | Private messages are always safe | Ellie never shares anything with anyone | Messages vanish forever once they have been read
test: «phones in the kitchen at 8, that's so mid». («mid» means boring or poor.) What did Sam find after two weeks? | He slept better and was less grumpy | He was more tired and grumpy than ever before | He failed all his tests | He made lots more enemies at school
test: What would probably happen if nobody showed a grown-up the photo of Sam asleep? | It would keep spreading and Sam would stay upset | It would vanish on its own in a day or so | Sam would find it funny later and post it himself | Everyone would forget by teatime
test: «he's our teacher, he'll never see the chat, lol». What really happened? | It got screenshotted and shown to grown-ups | He never found out | He joined the chat | The chat deleted itself
test: In a film, a kid gets a whole school to laugh at someone online and becomes popular. In real life what usually happens? | The poster gets in trouble and someone gets badly hurt | The poster becomes the most popular kid in the whole school | Nobody minds at all | The victim thanks the poster for the attention
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a «pile-on» in an online group? | A collective turning against one person which constitutes bullying | A harmless expression of collective opinion | A procedure for the fair election of a leader | A means by which friendships are strengthened
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate course of action for a pupil who becomes the subject of mockery in a class group chat. | To refrain from replying, preserve the evidence and inform an adult | To reply in kind with greater severity | To circulate unflattering images of the participants in return | To remain in the chat and read every message until it ceases
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning messages sent privately by electronic means. | They may be copied and disseminated by the recipient | They are incapable of being copied | They are automatically destroyed upon reading | They are protected from disclosure by law in all cases
exam: Identify the principal benefit, as established in the lesson, of removing telephones from bedrooms at night. | Improved sleep and fewer nocturnal disputes | Increased popularity among peers at school | Faster completion of homework at night | The complete prevention of all online bullying
